/* strings_unsafe_abi -- Future exact C ABI (SPEC ARCH-006, Appendix B.8).
 *
 * SS-203 (BL-103 / OQ-007): OQ-007 resolved upstream by sv0c's
 * FFI-001..015 slices (task/sv0c-ffi-raw-pointer-abi.Rmd) -- raw pointer
 * types, `unsafe { }` blocks, and `#[extern_c]` declarations now exist,
 * are checker-gated (E0550/E0551/E0552), and are stably rejected on the
 * VM backend (E0553) rather than silently miscompiled. This module is
 * the first real consumer.
 *
 * Every function here is a real, UNADAPTED C ABI call -- not a safe sv0
 * signature. The caller assumes every safety obligation the equivalent
 * libc call assumes (pointer validity, NUL-termination, no concurrent
 * mutation, lifetime for the call's duration, and so on) and must invoke
 * it from inside an `unsafe { }` block (SPEC Appendix B.8's own worked
 * example). Every function's own doc comment states its full caller
 * obligation (SPEC DOC-005) and doubles as sv0c's FFI-011 caller-
 * obligation lint fixture -- the parent sv0-toolchain repo's
 * `scripts/verify_extern_c_caller_obligations.py --root` already walks
 * this file (it `rglob`s every `.sv0` under the whole workspace, this
 * submodule included), so an undocumented `#[extern_c]` here fails that
 * gate exactly as it would in sv0c's own test corpus.
 *
 * ARCH-006 (feature-gated, separately versioned, excluded from the
 * default build): NOT yet a real build-time feature flag -- sv0 has no
 * such mechanism today, and building one is SS-204's own job, along
 * with the external-linkage test corpus proving these declared
 * signatures really link against a real C library (SS-203 is "write the
 * real functions"; SS-204 is "gate + prove real linkage" -- the division
 * task/sv0c-ffi-raw-pointer-abi.Rmd's own Epic F already draws). The one
 * piece that could NOT wait for SS-204: `scripts/test` / `scripts/
 * sanitize` / `scripts/path_order_corpus` all stage every top-level file
 * directly under lib (a flat wildcard copy, no subdirectories) into a
 * fresh project directory, and sv0's project mode
 * compiles every staged `.sv0` file whether imported or not (SPEC
 * ARCH-011) -- landing this file unguarded would put an `ItemExternFn`
 * in every single VM-backend `--project` build's own translation unit,
 * tripping the brand-new FFI-014 VM gate (E0553) on every existing,
 * otherwise-unrelated test. Those scripts now drop this one file from
 * the default stage (env-var opt-in: `SV0_STRINGS_INCLUDE_UNSAFE_ABI=1`)
 * -- a minimal, load-bearing slice of SS-204's own gate that had to land
 * atomically with this file, not the full feature-gate design SS-204
 * still owns.
 *
 * Never imported by any safe module (ARCH-006) -- no `strings_*` file in
 * this package references `strings_unsafe_abi`.
 */
module strings_unsafe_abi;

/// Caller obligation: `s` must be a valid, non-null pointer to a
/// NUL-terminated byte sequence; the pointed-to memory must remain valid
/// and must not be concurrently mutated by another thread for the
/// duration of the call. Mirrors libc's own `strlen(3)` contract exactly
/// -- this is the unadapted exact-ABI call, not `strings_cstr`'s safe
/// `CStr`-based length (which validates the invariant once at
/// construction, per MODEL-014, instead of trusting the caller).
#[extern_c]
fn strlen(s: *const byte) -> usize;
